WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:06.540
Captain America: Civil War lived up to all
of the hype, offering yet another spectacular

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.709
entry into the Marvel Cinematic Universe.
While the film felt more like a third Avengers

00:00:10.709 --> 00:00:14.510
film than the third Captain America flick,
we're still shocked at all the crazy plot

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:19.019
twists and superpowered fights. Amidst the
carnage, there are plenty of references and

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:22.720
Easter eggs that you may not have noticed
while you were gawking at your favorite superheroes

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.449
going all WWE on each other. As you'd expect,
there's a Wakanda-sized amount of spoilers

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:33.239
ahead, so you might want to put this list
on ice if you haven't seen the movie yet...

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:34.380
For the birds

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.870
In the comics, Sam Wilson usually has a pet
falcon named Redwing that he brings with him

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.180
to the battlefield.He has a telepathic link
with the bird that syncs their brains together,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.650
allowing Wilson to see whatever the falcon
sees. To make things simpler, Captain America:

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.640
Civil War made Redwing a remote controlled
drone that would fly out of his backpack.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:55.510
Wilson could use his goggles to see from the
perspective of the machine to get a tactical

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:59.740
readout on his surroundings. Gotta admit,
that's a lot cooler than an escapee from a

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.270
bird sanctuary.

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:03.290
Rearranging Crossbones

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.520
Holding a grudge against Steve Rogers for
the events of Captain America: The Winter

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.960
Soldier, Crossbones attempts to steal a biological
weapon in Lagos, Nigeria. Eventually, the

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.990
villain gets in close and blows himself up
alongside Cap in a heavily populated marketplace.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.780
Scarlet Witch's containment of the blast negates
a lot of the casualties that would've happened

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.549
otherwise, but some innocents get killed nonetheless,
triggering the government to step in with

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:29.249
the Sokovia Accords. While he may have inadvertently
helped start the Civil War in the movie, he

00:01:29.249 --> 00:01:33.759
does the opposite in the comics. After Steve
Rogers turns himself in to the authorities,

00:01:33.759 --> 00:01:39.200
mean ol' Crossbones snipes him from afar,
leading to Cap's death. But don't worry...he

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:39.700
got better.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.569
Dealing with Karpov by the book

00:01:41.569 --> 00:01:45.450
Remember that guy who had the red book with
the passwords that activated the Winter Soldier's

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:49.889
programming? That character is Vasily Karpov,
who played a slightly more important role

00:01:49.889 --> 00:01:54.049
in the comics. Karpov helped Cap foil one
of Red Skull's plans, but he also played a

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:58.299
pivotal role in turning an injured Bucky Barnes
into the Winter Soldier.

00:01:58.299 --> 00:01:59.850
Community contributions

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.799
Directors Anthony and Joseph Russo are known
for directing episodes of Arrested Development

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.950
and Community prior to their adventures in
the Marvel Cinematic Universe. In Civil War,

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.670
Jim Rash, known for playing Dean Craig Pelton
on Community, makes a cameo in CapDEAN America:

00:02:13.670 --> 00:02:18.500
Civil War. He played the MIT liaison who was
speaking to Tony Stark after his on-stage

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:22.840
announcement. If you check out the Russos'
previous Marvel movie, The Winter Soldier,

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.959
you may have recognized Abed himself, Danny
Pudi, as an agent of S.H.I.E.L.D. Cool as

00:02:26.959 --> 00:02:30.849
that is, we're still hoping he shows up on
the big screen as a different comic character

00:02:30.849 --> 00:02:31.909
someday...

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:34.180
"Batman"

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.980
"Ya"

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:37.069
Excellent Vision

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:40.900
In the comics, the Vision started wearing
clothes so he wouldn't freak out his other

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.349
teammates as much, and try to blend in more.
We saw Paul Bettany decked out in sweaters

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:49.530
throughout Civil War, while he was still wearing
the Mind Stone in his forehead, which makes

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.390
for one awesome fashion accessory. Furthermore,
we saw the comic-based relationship between

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:58.680
Vision and Wanda continue to flourish from
its start in Age of Ultron, as the two characters

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:03.030
seemingly care for one another, despite how
weird and chaotic their lives have been so

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:03.319
far.

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:04.329
Moving words

00:03:04.329 --> 00:03:08.159
We're sure a lot of you got a little teary
eyed seeing Steve Rogers mourn as he carried

00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:12.709
Agent Peggy Carter's coffin at her funeral.
Captain America's former neighbor/potential

00:03:12.709 --> 00:03:18.340
love interest Sharon Carter gives a heartfelt
eulogy for her aunt that might sound familiar.

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.379
In the comics, Captain America explains his
decisions with the iconic "No, you move" speech.

00:03:23.379 --> 00:03:27.510
In the Marvel Cinematic Universe, Cap hears
it from Sharon at a time when he needed to

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.209
hear it most, cementing his resolve and stance
on the Sokovia Accords.

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:33.609
May I buy you a drink?

00:03:33.609 --> 00:03:37.700
Sure, it was a little awkward watching Tony
Stark hanging out and kind of flirting with

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.840
Aunt May before meeting Peter Parker. But
this isn't the first time Aunt May got cozy

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:46.739
with Tony Stark. The 1994 romantic comedy
Only You featured Downey and Tomei falling

00:03:46.739 --> 00:03:50.569
in love and all that good stuff by the end
of the film. Is it possible that Iron Man

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:53.060
might become Spider-Man's uncle in a few movies?

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.180
Iron Spider

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.049
Spider-Man originally played an even larger
part in the comic book version of Civil War.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:02.200
Siding with Tony Stark and supporting the
Superhuman Registration Act, Spider-Man reveals

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.900
to the world that he is actually Peter Parker,
resulting in all kinds of problems in the

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:10.560
long run for the wall-crawler. Fortunately,
Tony Stark backs him throughout most of the

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:15.239
events, even making a new, armored, "Iron
Spider" suit that featured extra arms and

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:20.389
all kinds of cool gadgets. During the movie,
Stark finds Peter's original, makeshift superhero

00:04:20.389 --> 00:04:24.120
outfit a little underwhelming, since he's
only been your friendly neighborhood Spider-Man

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.340
for a few months by this time. He ultimately
gives him his first real costume, complete

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:29.889
with some new toys.

00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:31.050
Ant and Hawk

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:35.310
During the film's fight between the two opposing
halves of The Avengers, a miniaturized Ant-Man

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:39.509
gets boosted toward Iron Man by holding onto
one of Hawkeye's arrows. This scene is actually

00:04:39.509 --> 00:04:45.030
inspired by The Avengers issue #223, which
depicts the awesome maneuver on its cover.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:49.710
But the fight only gets better when Ant-Man
takes a huge gamble and becomes...

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.789
Giant-Man

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:55.180
In order to create a big, distracting diversion,
instead of shrinking to roughly half an inch

00:04:55.180 --> 00:05:00.280
in height, Ant-Man tries a new trick and turns
ginormous. What non-comics fans may not have

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.300
realized is that Ant-Man has a long history
of skipping the small stuff and going big.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:11.819
He first shrunk down to fun-size proportions
as Ant-Man in 1962's Tales to Astonish #35.

00:05:11.819 --> 00:05:17.849
By Tales to Astonish #49 in 1963, he made
his debut as Giant-Man, and fought a lame

00:05:17.849 --> 00:05:22.180
villain named the Living Eraser. Let's hope
Marvel never decides to bring that dude into

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.110
the movies...

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:24.599
Trading places

00:05:24.599 --> 00:05:28.780
Seeing War Machine get accidentally shot down
by Vision and lose the use of his legs was

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:33.360
pretty sad. These events sort of mirror what
happened between Tony Stark and James Rhodes

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.259
in the comics. After being shot and paralyzed,
Tony Stark uses technology to help himself

00:05:38.259 --> 00:05:42.740
walk and move around. Tony's overall health
started to worsen, resulting in him creating

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.880
the Variable Threat Assessment Armor, which
would become the War Machine armor. After

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:51.910
everyone thought he died, Tony Stark's will
named Rhodes the head of his company and gave

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.400
him the new suit. Now, War Machine has become
the one struggling to walk after being shot,

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.710
which shows a sad but unique twist on things.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:05:59.930
The Raft

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:04.120
Meant to hold supervillains who are too dangerous
for normal jail cells, the Raft's island-like

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.139
designs were inspired by Alcatraz. In the
movie, the government takes things a step

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:11.410
further by making The Raft entirely submersible.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:12.810
The climactic fight

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:17.669
The movie's title fight breaks down to a slugfest
featuring Captain America and Bucky vs. Iron

00:06:17.669 --> 00:06:22.050
Man. After Bucky gets disarmed, you know,
like when he loses his arm, Captain America

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:26.639
and Iron Man go all-out in a one-on-one brawl.
The fight features a highlighted segment where

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.199
Tony Stark fires his repulsor blasts into
Captain America's shield, recreating the iconic

00:06:31.199 --> 00:06:33.530
comic book cover on the big screen.

00:06:33.530 --> 00:06:34.879
Back to basics

00:06:34.879 --> 00:06:38.580
After scanning and predicting Captain America's
fighting techniques in record time, Iron Man

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.150
proceeds to beat him bloody with relative
ease. After getting trampled, Steve Rogers

00:06:43.150 --> 00:06:46.170
struggles to get up, and we have a familiar
exchange:

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.620
"I could do this all day"

00:06:48.629 --> 00:06:53.050
If that sounds familiar, there's a good reason:
Steve said the same thing in a pre-Super Soldier

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:56.530
Serum scene in Captain America: The First
Avenger:

00:06:56.530 --> 00:06:58.930
"I could do this all day"

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.680
The Spider Signal

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.710
The second post-credits scene ends up with
Peter Parker nursing his wounds back home

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.860
in Queens while toying around with the new
web shooters Tony Stark gave him. He accidentally

00:07:12.860 --> 00:07:17.090
shines a bright red light from the device,
which turns out to be the Spider Signal. Old

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:21.220
school comic fans should definitely be happy
to see this calling card of the amazing wall-crawler.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:26.310
Basically, Spider-Man would shine this giant
projection of his face onto unsuspecting villains,

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:30.699
announcing his entrance before he swings in
to save the day. Doing that might make for

00:07:30.699 --> 00:07:34.150
a more dramatic entrance than this:

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.080
"Hey everyone"

00:07:38.069 --> 00:07:41.620
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to see more videos like the one you

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.860
just saw. And leave us a comment to let us
know which Civil War Easter eggs we missed...

